WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.250
NET and ASP.

2
00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:06.374
NET Core use a configuration system that can combine values from multiple sources.

3
00:00:06.374 --> 00:00:20.122
Configuration files, environment variables, development user secrets, app platform settings, command-line inputs, and secret stores can all influence runtime behavior depending on how the application is built and hosted.

4
00:00:20.122 --> 00:00:26.934
Connection strings are commonly placed under a ConnectionStrings section and read through configuration APIs.

5
00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:30.933
That convention is useful, but it is not a protection mechanism.

6
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:41.244
A value under that section is still only as safe as the source that supplied it, the people and systems that can read it, and the logs and tools that may display it.

7
00:00:41.244 --> 00:00:47.244
Developers should understand which source wins when the same key appears in more than one place.

8
00:00:47.244 --> 00:00:48.993
Configuration order matters.

9
00:00:48.993 --> 00:00:55.555
A value from one source may override a file value, and a deployment setting may override a local default.

10
00:00:55.555 --> 00:01:01.742
Review should include the expected source for each environment, not only the key name used in code.

11
00:01:01.742 --> 00:01:08.241
Environment-specific configuration should separate development, testing, staging, and production values.

12
00:01:08.241 --> 00:01:19.364
The goal is to make it difficult to run a local build against production data, deploy a test connection to production, or copy a shared secret across environments without review.

13
00:01:19.364 --> 00:01:24.364
Sample configuration files should use placeholders rather than sensitive values.

14
00:01:24.364 --> 00:01:35.300
Onboarding docs, examples, local setup scripts, and checked-in templates should teach the shape of the configuration without publishing usable credentials or internal details.

15
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:50.610
A secure configuration review asks where the connection string comes from, which source has precedence, who can change that source, where the value may be displayed, and how the application proves it is using the intended environment at runtime.
